The exception hierarchy:

```java
// java.lang.Object
//   └── java.lang.Throwable
//         ├── java.lang.Error              ← UNCHECKED (JVM errors — don't catch!)
//         │     ├── OutOfMemoryError
//         │     ├── StackOverflowError
//         │     └── VirtualMachineError
//         └── java.lang.Exception           ← CHECKED (must handle!)
//               ├── IOException
//               ├── SQLException
//               ├── FileNotFoundException
//               └── java.lang.RuntimeException  ← UNCHECKED (don't have to handle)
//                     ├── NullPointerException
//                     ├── IllegalArgumentException
//                     ├── IllegalStateException
//                     ├── IndexOutOfBoundsException
//                     └── ClassCastException
```

CHECKED exceptions — compiler forces handling:

```java
// ═══ CHECKED: IOException — compiler forces try-catch or throws ═══
public String readTradeFile(String path) throws IOException {
    // (Avoid) Without throws or try-catch: COMPILE ERROR!
    // "Unhandled exception: java.io.IOException"
    return Files.readString(Path.of(path));
}

// Caller MUST handle:
try {
    String data = readTradeFile("/trades/daily.csv");
} catch (IOException e) {
    log.error("Failed to read trade file: {}", e.getMessage());
    // Handle: retry, fallback, alert
}

// OR declare throws (pass to caller):
public void processTradeFile() throws IOException {
    String data = readTradeFile("/trades/daily.csv");
    // IOException passed up — CALLER must now handle it!
}
// This "throws chain" can propagate up 5-10 methods — BOILERPLATE!
```

UNCHECKED exceptions — runtime, no forced handling:

```java
// ═══ UNCHECKED: NullPointerException — no compile-time check ═══
public BigDecimal getTradeAmount(Trade trade) {
    return trade.getAmount();  // if trade is null → NullPointerException at RUNTIME!
    // Compiler doesn't force you to catch NullPointerException.
    // FIX: validate input, don't catch the symptom!
}

// ═══ UNCHECKED: IllegalArgumentException — programming error ═══
public void withdraw(BigDecimal amount) {
    if (amount.signum() <= 0) {
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("Amount must be positive: " + amount);
        // No "throws" in method signature needed!
        // Caller is NOT forced to catch it.
        // The caller should validate BEFORE calling withdraw.
    }
    balance = balance.subtract(amount);
}
```

The argument AGAINST checked exceptions:

```java
// PROBLEM 1: Boilerplate everywhere
public void processAllTrades() {
    try {
        String data = readFile();        // throws IOException
        List<Trade> trades = parse(data); // throws ParseException
        validate(trades);                 // throws ValidationException
        persist(trades);                  // throws SQLException
    } catch (IOException | ParseException | ValidationException | SQLException e) {
        throw new RuntimeException("Trade processing failed", e);
        // MOST code just wraps checked in unchecked anyway!
    }
}

// PROBLEM 2: Signature pollution — throws chain propagates up
public void controllerMethod() throws IOException, ParseException, SQLException { }
// Every method in the chain must declare throws — ugly, brittle.

// PROBLEM 3: Lambdas can't throw checked exceptions!
trades.stream()
    .map(t -> {
        try {
            return externalApi.settle(t);  // throws SettlementException (checked)
        } catch (SettlementException e) {
            throw new RuntimeException(e);  // wrap in unchecked — ugly!
        }
    })
    .toList();
// If SettlementException were unchecked → clean lambda, no try-catch.

// MODERN APPROACH: Spring wraps all checked in unchecked
// SQLException → DataAccessException (unchecked!)
// IOException → UncategorizedIOException (unchecked!)
// Handle with @ExceptionHandler / @ControllerAdvice → global, clean, one place.
```

When checked exceptions ARE appropriate:

```java
// Checked exceptions ARE useful when:
// 1. The caller CAN and SHOULD take a specific recovery action
//    (FileNotFoundException → try another file path)
// 2. The exception is EXPECTED and RECOVERABLE
//    (InterruptedException → restore interrupt flag)
// 3. API design requires caller awareness
//    (close() throws IOException → try-with-resources handles it)

// Checked exceptions are NOT useful when:
// 1. Caller just wraps in RuntimeException anyway
// 2. Exception represents a bug (should be unchecked)
// 3. Used in lambda/stream context (breaks functional style)
```

